<Base title="Planning and executing a PostgreSQL query" description="Explain parameter-sensitive prepared plans, early results, blocking operations, spills and parallel memory demand with measured PostgreSQL examples.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
 <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Inside PostgreSQL</p><h1>Planning and executing a query</h1><TitleLine type="Deep dive" version="PostgreSQL 18" /></header>
<ArticleBody>
 <p>A query plan describes work the server can perform to obtain an answer. It is chosen before the executor knows the actual result, using estimates about the data and the available access paths. The executor then runs that plan, asking its operations for rows and keeping whatever intermediate state they need. Separating these two moments explains several common surprises: a prepared query can be fast for one customer and slow for another, five returned rows can require reading an entire table, and one statement can use several independent memory allowances.</p>
 <p>We will use a deliberately uneven job table. Tenant 0 owns 45,000 of its 50,000 records; each of tenants 1–100 owns 50. We will first change what the planner knows about the requested tenant, then change whether the executor can produce the first five jobs without examining all the others. The experiments were run on PostgreSQL 18.6. The browser lessons reproduce the parameter and demand comparisons in PGlite; parallel workers and server I/O need a server.</p>
 <section id="before"><h2>Parsing, rewriting and planning</h2>
 <p>Parsing recognises the statement’s syntax. Analysis resolves names, types and operators against database catalogues. Rewriting transforms the query where required, including expanding views. The planner then constructs alternative paths for obtaining the requested result and compares their estimated costs. Execution follows the selected tree of operations. A prepared statement lets PostgreSQL retain earlier work between calls, but it does not necessarily freeze one execution plan forever. These stages are described in the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/query-path.html">query-processing overview</a>.</p>
 <p>The estimates answer questions such as how many rows a predicate will select and how much work an index route will save. Statistics are summaries, not a live enumeration of every possible result. If most rows belong to one tenant, knowing the tenant’s actual value can matter far more than knowing that the predicate is an integer equality. “There is an index on tenant” does not settle whether following it will be economical.</p>
 <details><summary>Create the uneven table on a server</summary><p>Use a disposable database. Run the setup outside an explicit transaction, then use the same connection for the prepared-statement commands. To repeat, deallocate the prepared statement if it exists, drop <code>core_jobs</code> and recreate it. The browser lessons have their own independent setup.</p><Sql code={setup} label="50,000 jobs with one large tenant" /></details>
 <p>The recorded server comparison disables parallel workers in its test session with <code>max_parallel_workers_per_gather = 0</code>, keeping the initial plans serial. Restore it with <code>RESET max_parallel_workers_per_gather</code> when finished. Parallel execution adds resource choices that we examine later.</p>
 <p>The payload makes each table row wider than its tenant index entry. It is intentionally selected by the tenant query so that the index cannot satisfy the whole result by itself. A small tenant needs 50 matching heap rows. The large tenant needs 45,000. Reading the table once can be a better route to the latter answer than visiting almost all of it through an index.</p>
 <p>This chapter assumes the meaning of scans, joins and plan estimates from <a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-execution/">How queries are executed</a>. Here the extra question is how PostgreSQL’s planning and execution lifecycle makes those operations behave inside a repeated application request.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="parameters"><h2>Custom and generic plans</h2>
 <Sql code={prepare} label="Prepare a tenant lookup in the current session" />
 <p>The parameter <code>$1</code> will be supplied at execution. A <strong>custom plan</strong> can use that particular value when estimating the work. A <strong>generic plan</strong> must serve executions with different values, so its choice cannot depend on whether this call names tenant 0 or tenant 1. Reusing a generic plan saves repeated planning work, but the same route may be a poor fit for a skewed parameter distribution.</p>
 <p>Force custom plans temporarily to expose the distinction. Predict which request is likely to use the tenant index before running the commands.</p>
 <Sql code={custom} label="Plan separately for the small and large tenants" />
 <p>Our server chose an Index Scan for tenant 1 and returned 50 rows. For tenant 0 it chose a Sequential Scan and returned 45,000. These are both reasonable uses of the available information: the first predicate eliminates almost everything, while the second needs nearly the whole table. A sequential scan here is evidence of a broad request, not evidence that the index is broken.</p>
 <Sql code={generic} label="Use one parameter-independent plan for both values" />
 <p>Both generic executions used the tenant index in our run. The plan estimated 495 rows for the scan, showed <code>tenant_id = $1</code> in its condition, and actually returned 50 or 45,000 according to the supplied value. The plan’s independence is visible in that unchanged estimate. The large request does not become small because the generic plan was chosen for a much smaller estimated answer.</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Observed parameter-sensitive plans"><table><thead><tr><th>Planning mode</th><th>Tenant</th><th>Observed route</th><th>Actual rows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Custom</td><td>1</td><td>Index Scan</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>Custom</td><td>0</td><td>Sequential Scan</td><td>45,000</td></tr><tr><td>Generic</td><td>1</td><td>Index Scan, estimated 495</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>Generic</td><td>0</td><td>Index Scan, estimated 495</td><td>45,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 <p>The forced modes are a diagnostic comparison. Under the default automatic policy, PostgreSQL initially uses five custom plans, compares their average estimated cost with a generic alternative, and decides whether repeated planning is worthwhile. It is not benchmarking both strategies against measured request latency on every call. Updated statistics and relevant definition changes can also invalidate cached planning work. See the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-prepare.html">prepared-statement rules</a> for that lifecycle.</p>
 <p>This gives a useful investigation when a few tenants have much worse latency. Reproduce representative small and large parameter values, inspect the prepared statement’s plan in the same session, and compare estimates with actual work. Testing a literal SQL statement in a fresh connection may use a custom plan and miss the generic-plan behaviour of the application. Conversely, an application sending parameters is not proof that its driver retains a named prepared statement; verify what that driver and pool actually do.</p>
 <p>Change the distribution as a second experiment: give every tenant the same number of jobs, analyse the table and compare again. The special advantage of knowing tenant 0 should diminish because it is no longer exceptional. That is a more useful conclusion than adopting <code>force_custom_plan</code> globally: first establish whether parameter-sensitive work is the cause, and weigh the execution saving against repeated planning.</p>
 <p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-plan-parameters">Try the parameter-sensitive plan lesson →</a> It includes both parameter values, the forced comparison and an equal-size variation.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="demand"><h2>Streaming and blocking execution</h2>
 <p>The executor runs a tree of plan nodes. In the ordinary row-at-a-time model, a parent asks a child for another result, and the child does the work needed to supply it. Some nodes can respond incrementally. Others need substantial input before their first output. A Limit node can stop asking after five rows, but this does not guarantee that the work below it was limited to five rows.</p>
 <p>Each fixture job has <code>priority = (job_id * 37) % 101</code>. Lower numbers come first. Multiples of 101 therefore have priority zero, and ordering those ties by job ID makes 101, 202, 303, 404 and 505 the first five.</p>
 <Sql code={top} label="Find five jobs with the lowest priority number" />
 <p>Initially we have no index on priority. To know which five jobs come first, PostgreSQL must consider every candidate. Our observed plan was Limit above Sort above Sequential Scan. The scan supplied 50,000 rows. The sort used a top-N heapsort, retaining a small best-so-far set rather than storing the complete sorted result. That saves memory, but a later input could still displace an earlier candidate, so all 50,000 must be examined before the first answer is known.</p>
 <p>Now give the executor a way to encounter candidates in the requested order. The second key breaks priority ties deterministically.</p>
 <Sql code={index} label="Store the complete requested ordering" />
 <p>Run the same EXPLAIN again. The verified plan was Limit above Index Only Scan, with five rows produced by the scan and no separate sort. Both plans returned job IDs 101, 202, 303, 404 and 505, all priority zero. The result stayed the same; the input needed to establish it changed.</p>
 <PlanDemand />
 <p>The word “only” in that scan’s name describes an available index-based route, not a guarantee of zero heap work. Our newly populated table still required visibility checks against heap pages. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/">Vacuum and the visibility map</a> explain when those checks can be avoided. For this comparison, the important observation is that the scan stopped after producing five rows instead of consuming the table.</p>
 <p>Add a selective condition on a field outside the ordering index and predict what changes. The executor may have to walk past many ordered entries before finding five that pass the filter. An ordered route makes early stopping possible; it does not guarantee that the first five entries satisfy every condition. For an application, the relevant measure is the work needed to produce five accepted rows.</p>
 <p>Similar startup requirements occur elsewhere. A conventional hash join builds its hash table before probing it; a full sort needs its input before output; an aggregate producing one grand total must consume all contributing rows. An incremental sort can release a completed group earlier when its input already has part of the required order. Read each node’s actual input and startup behaviour rather than inferring work from the final row count. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/executor.html">executor description</a> explains the parent/child model.</p>
 <p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-executor-demand">Try the five-row demand lesson →</a> Compare the two plans, then add a filter that makes the ordered scan reject earlier entries.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="memory"><h2>Working memory and parallel execution</h2>
 <p>A top-N sort keeping five narrow records needs little state. Sorting all 50,000 payloads is a different task. The executor needs a way to arrange the full input, and its working-memory allowance can be too small to keep the intermediate data in memory. It then uses temporary storage. The final answer is still correct; the route now includes extra I/O and merging work.</p>
 <Sql code={spill} label="Observe a full sort with a deliberately small allowance" />
 <p>Our run reported an external merge sort with disk space, along with temporary blocks in the buffer statistics. The 64 KiB setting makes this small fixture demonstrate a spill. It is local to the explicit transaction and is restored when the transaction ends. Increase it experimentally and inspect the sort method again, keeping the query and data constant. Compare repeated measurements rather than treating one elapsed time as a storage benchmark.</p>
 <p><code>work_mem</code> is an allowance for individual operations, not one total budget for a connection or a whole query. A plan can have several sorts or hashes, and several sessions can run such plans together. Hash operations also use <code>hash_mem_multiplier</code> when deriving their allowance. A change from 4 MiB to 64 MiB therefore has consequences beyond the one spilling sort that motivated it. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html">resource settings</a> describe these accounting boundaries.</p>
 <p>For a capacity estimate, suppose ten concurrent requests can each have two independent 64 MiB operations active. That represents roughly 1.25 GiB of potential working memory before other executor state, connections, shared buffers or operating-system needs. It is an illustrative bound on those operations, not a prediction that every node allocates its full allowance simultaneously. Inspect the actual plan shapes and concurrency before multiplying a configuration value.</p>
 <p>Parallel execution adds another dimension. A Gather node receives rows from a parallel portion of a plan; Gather Merge preserves ordered streams as it combines them. A parallel-aware scan divides work so participants contribute disjoint parts of the result. Some operations remain private to each participant, while mechanisms such as parallel hash can share coordinated state. The leader also has work to do, including consuming worker output. More workers can reduce elapsed time while increasing total CPU and memory demand.</p>
 <p>In particular, per-worker memory limits mean that a plan with a leader and four workers may have much greater resource use than its serial form. The exact multiplication depends on which operations execute where and whether state is shared. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/parallel-plans.html">parallel-plan documentation</a> is useful when tracing the tree below Gather. A browser’s single embedded execution environment is not a faithful capacity model for that server plan.</p>
 <p>PostgreSQL 18 also introduces asynchronous I/O for supported paths, including sequential and bitmap heap scans. It can overlap outstanding reads instead of waiting for each one serially. That can improve a path’s throughput without changing the number of rows the query requires, the sort’s blocking requirement or an inappropriate parameter estimate. Its benefit depends on the I/O method, workload and storage; a warm five-row lookup is not a demonstration of the feature. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/release-18.html">PostgreSQL 18 release notes</a> identify the supported operations.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="measure"><h2>Query plans and total request time</h2>
 <p>EXPLAIN ANALYZE executes the statement and records executor observations. For a modifying statement, that means real changes unless the surrounding transaction is rolled back. The plan’s costs are estimates in planner units; its actual times and rows are execution measurements. A parent includes work performed below it, so adding every displayed node time double-counts work. Repeated nodes report per-loop averages, which need their loop counts to understand total row processing.</p>
 <p>Even a carefully read plan does not include the application’s complete experience. Waiting for a pool connection happens before the statement reaches its backend. Transmitting and consuming a large result takes work beyond finding the rows. An application that fetches 45,000 payloads has chosen a substantial result even if the server locates them efficiently. Diagnose the request boundary and then the server operation responsible for the delay.</p>
 <p>The experiments give three distinct tests. When parameter values change the amount of work, compare custom and generic plans. When a small result arrives late, inspect which node must consume input before producing it. When an isolated query improves after a memory increase, check whether the same change remains safe at realistic concurrency and worker counts. Each test changes one condition and asks whether the observed mechanism predicts the result.</p>
 </section>
